			<!--Section text--->
			<div class="text">
				Modular Cloning, or MoClo, is a relatively new assembly method based on Golden Gate introduced in 2011 by Ernst Weber et al., which uses Type IIS restriction enzymes (we used BpiI and BsaI) to generate 4bp overhangs. These 4bp overhangs are called fusion sites, which must match between parts in order to ligate them together.
				<br><br>
				This allows the user to ligate up to six DNA parts together in a one-pot reaction, cutting down the time it takes to build large circuits dramatically (shown below).
				<br><br>

				<!--Half width subsection ---->
				<div class="subsection half">
					<img src="https://2020.igem.org/wiki/images/f/f1/BioBricks_Assembly_9day_BU12.png" align="top">
				</div>

				<!--Half width subsection ---->
				<div class="subsection half">
					<img src="https://2020.igem.org/wiki/images/c/c6/MoClo_Assembly_3day_BU12.png" align="top">
				</div>

				<!--Place a clear to make sure the next part starts on a new line  ---->
				<div class="clear"></div>

				There are three Levels of MoClo Parts (shown below):
				<br><br>
				<b>Level 0:</b> Basic part (ex: promoter, RBS, CDS, etc.)<br>
				<b>Level 1:</b> Transcriptional unit (up to 6 Level 0 Parts)<br>
				<b>Level 2:</b> Composite of up to 6 Level 1 parts
				<br><br>

				<img src="https://2020.igem.org/wiki/images/0/0b/MoClo_Assembly_details_BU12.png">
				<br><br>
